WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.957
Detection is the point where the organization notices something that might be an incident.

2
00:00:06.107 --> 00:00:12.490
That signal might come from a security alert, an endpoint tool, cloud monitoring, identity logs,

3
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:20.300
a user report, a vendor notification, threat intelligence, a data loss signal, or an unusual business process event.

4
00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:23.429
Detection is not the same as confirmation.

5
00:00:23.579 --> 00:00:26.983
It is a prompt to ask better questions.

6
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:29.333
What happened?

7
00:00:29.483 --> 00:00:33.313
Which system, user, service, or data set is involved?

8
00:00:33.463 --> 00:00:36.867
Is the activity expected, suspicious, or clearly harmful?

9
00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:41.698
Good detection provides context so analysts can prioritize the next step.

10
00:00:41.848 --> 00:00:47.380
A useful alert should point toward relevant evidence instead of just raising alarm.

11
00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:56.040
Weak detection creates delays and uncertainty: responders may learn about an incident late, lack the logs needed to scope it,

12
00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.744
or spend time chasing noisy signals.

13
00:00:58.894 --> 00:01:07.404
Detection engineering should therefore focus on meaningful coverage, clear alert descriptions, useful enrichment, and routing to people who can act.

14
00:01:07.554 --> 00:01:10.959
The goal is not to alert on everything.

15
00:01:11.109 --> 00:01:19.194
The goal is to notice important signals early enough that analysis and containment still have time to reduce impact.

16
00:01:19.344 --> 00:01:21.544
Detection also benefits from feedback.

17
00:01:21.694 --> 00:01:27.226
If analysts repeatedly close an alert as noise, the detection may need tuning.

18
00:01:27.376 --> 00:01:32.907
If responders keep asking for context that is not present, enrichment should improve.

19
00:01:33.057 --> 00:01:41.568
The best detection program learns from incidents, near misses, user reports, and false positives rather than treating alerts as fixed

20
00:01:41.718 --> 00:01:43.918
forever.
